\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Ancient DNA has given us a window beyond fossils, allowing us to track how phenotypes changed in ways that don\hich\f31506 \rquote \loch\f31506 
t show up in the fossil record. After humans, horses are one of the species ancient genomics has studied the most. In fact, the very first succe\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
ssful recovery and analysis of ancient DNA was from 150-year-old tissue from the extinct quagga, a close relative of the zebra, in 1984 \hich\f31506 \endash \loch\f31506 
