\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 The need for speed
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
By the seventeenth century, horses had been serving human purposes for thousands of years, plowing fields, carrying soldiers, and pulling carts. But in early-modern England, one goal came to dominate: speed. In the 1660s, King Charles II, a fervent horse 
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
racing fan, set up a horse breeding and racing center at Newmarket, near London. While horse racing had been popular in previous centuries, Charles II and other racing enthusiasts were the first to methodically breed horses at scale to improve their race 
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 pe\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 rformance.
\par \~
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 A 1671 illustration of a race at Newmarket Heath. Image credit: Yale Center for British Art.
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
Breeders discovered that horses from Arabia, Central Asia, and North Africa were often faster: they were lighter, had a greater drive to run, and were better able to dissipate heat, allowing them to sustain high speeds without overheating. From three exce
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 ptional horses imported to England, Byerley Turk (imported in the 1680s), Darley Arabian (1704), and Godolphin Arabian (1729), breeders created the \hich\f31506 \lquote \loch\f31506 thoroughbred\hich\f31506 \rquote 
\loch\f31506 , a new type of horse designed for speed. Today, 95 percent of all living thoroughbreds are t\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 ho\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
ught to descend from just one of these stallions, Darley Arabian, through his phenomenally successful great-great-grandson, Eclipse.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Horse breeding used to be guesswork: breeders paired fast stallions with fast mares and hoped for the best. But in 1791, the General Stud Book started recording every thoroughbred\hich\f31506 \rquote \loch\f31506 
s ancestry, turning pedigree into data. Breeders could now track bloodlines\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
, deliberately plan matings, and see which combinations actually worked. Thoroughbreds today are defined as horses whose ancestry can be traced entirely to horses listed in the General Stud Book.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Harder, better, faster, stronger
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Racing and breeding evolved side by side. Races in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries involved multiple heats over distances of two to four miles, favoring horses with stamina. Horses didn\hich\f31506 \rquote 
\loch\f31506 t race until they were five or six years old. But by the lat\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
e eighteenth century, new races like the Derby and the St Leger reset the terms of competition. Horses now raced at three years old, over shorter distances of one to two miles.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
This new format meant an increased premium on speed and precocity, and it has persisted, particularly in the United States and Australia. In Britain and Ireland, the tradition of long-distance races held on in parallel, leading to distinct types: horses t
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 hat specialize in explosive speed at a young age, classic middle-distance horses, and horses bred for the stamina needed to excel in longer races.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
After the creation of the thoroughbred and the Stud Book, Anglo-American breeders hit what appears to be an upper limit for speed surprisingly quickly. From the middle of the nineteenth century, when timing and record keeping became good enough to make re
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
liable comparisons, race times improved steadily for about half a century. But although there was consistent improvement, it was small: times only fell by 1 to 2 percent, or 2 to 4 seconds over a 1.5-mile race. Race times for prominent races have not sign
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 if\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 icantly fallen since around 1910. Secretariat\hich\f31506 \rquote \loch\f31506 
s 1973 Belmont time eclipsed the previous record by a stunning 2.6 seconds. However, the best time since then (Easy Goer in 1989 at 2 minutes 26 seconds) was a full 2 seconds slower than Secretariat\hich\f31506 \rquote \loch\f31506 s.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
Outside the elite, there has been continued improvement: one study found that between 1997 and 2012, accounting for differences such as ground softness and the number of runners, the average British thoroughbred increased its speed by 0.011 yards per seco
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 nd per year. So while we have likely reached the ceiling for how fast an exceptional horse can run, the median horse is beginning to close in on that limit, too.
\par \~
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 The genes for speed
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
Until 2007, the Stud Book was the cutting edge of horse breeding technology. That changed when scientists sequenced a horse genome for the first time, that of a thoroughbred mare named Twilight. This allowed researchers to study the genetic basis of varia
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 tion between individual horses far more systematically.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
Another step forward in understanding thoroughbred genetics came with a paper published in 2010 that investigated the gene in horses that codes for myostatin, a protein that regulates muscle development. Mice, cattle, dogs, and even humans with mutations 
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 in this gene grow outlandishly large muscles because the gene normally acts as a brake on muscle development; with that brake damaged, muscles continue to grow unchecked.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
The gene coding for myostatin in horses has around 1,100 base pairs. Looking at the exact sequence of this gene, called MSTN, in 148 thoroughbreds revealed that horses could have three different versions of the gene, differing by just one of those base pa
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 irs (either a letter \hich\f31506 \lquote \loch\f31506 C\hich\f31506 \rquote \loch\f31506  or a \hich\f31506 \lquote \loch\f31506 T\hich\f31506 \rquote \loch\f31506 
), and that each version was associated with success in different race formats. Horses with two copies of the \hich\f31506 \lquote \loch\f31506 C\hich\f31506 \rquote \loch\f31506  version tend to be the best sprinters, those with two \hich\f31506 \lquote 
\loch\f31506 T\hich\f31506 \rquote \loch\f31506 s tend to be high-stamina long-distance runners, an\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 d \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 those with one of each tend to fall between the two running profiles.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Later research found that variations in MSTN correspond with different muscle physiology. Horses with the \hich\f31506 \lquote \loch\f31506 C\hich\f31506 \rquote \loch\f31506 
