oughbred genomes during the late eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, as English races like the Derby and St Leger got shorter. But the genetic trend is less pronounced in Britain than the United States, where races are rarely longer than 2,000 meters, an
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 d \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 especially than in Australia, which favors 1,000 to 1,400-meter lengths. Commercial tests for this gene are now widely used by breeders and trainers.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 The price of success
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Horses\hich\f31506 \rquote \loch\f31506 
 genetic diversity remained large for thousands of years, before collapsing after the introduction of modern breeding. In nature, diversity is maintained by competing selection pressures that balance one trait against another. A wild horse does not
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506  just need to be fast. Its bones need to last. It needs to be resistant to disease. Even a domesticated working horse would, before intensive breeding began, face natural constraints on its traits.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
In modern thoroughbred breeding, celebrity stallions with the best race records are in high demand. Their owners charge hundreds of thousands of dollars in stud fees, and they can sire perhaps a hundred foals per year. This means that the genes of particu
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 lar males can quickly sweep to dominance in a population in a way that would be very unusual in nature.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
Along with this loss of diversity, modern horses have accumulated harmful mutations as a result of breeding within a small population. In horses as in humans, repeated inbreeding concentrates harmful traits as parents who are related to each other are mor
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 e likely to share the same mutations, resulting in offspring with two copies of the mutated gene, rather than one mutated copy and one normal copy, which could mask the mutation\hich\f31506 \rquote \loch\f31506 
s effects.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
A genetic analysis of all Australian thoroughbreds, more than 135,000 horses, showed that inbreeding correlates with slower, shorter, and less lucrative careers. Amazingly, ten heavily crossed eighteenth-century ancestors account for over 80 percent of in
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 breeding in Australian thoroughbreds. Breeders are not blind to these risks. The American Quarter Horse Association, for example, requires breeding stallions to be tested for several genetic diseases.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 As genetics has played such a large role in breeding horses, it is small wonder that horse breeders are moving with the frontiers of science. The world\hich\f31506 \rquote \loch\f31506 
s first cloned horse, called Prometea, was born in 2003. Horse cloning is now big business, despite an \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 American Jockey Club ban, as other equine sports, including show jumping and polo, allow clones.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
In 2024, the Argentinian company Kheiron announced the production of the first CRISPR-edited horses: Polo Pureza, an award-winning mare, was the foundation for five gene-edited clones with increased explosive speed. According to reports, the company modif
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 ied the MSTN gene, so that the animals would develop more muscle fibers and potentially run faster as a result. Even so, the prospects for exceeding current records through gene editing are slim. Today\hich\f31506 
\rquote \loch\f31506 s racehorses are already tightly optimized for speed,\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506  b\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
alancing strength against the cost of carrying additional muscle mass. Adding more muscle is relatively low-hanging fruit, and if this were the key to unlocking greater speed, selective breeding would likely already have brought it about.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 The thoroughbred paradox
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 At the Belmont Stakes in 1973, Secretariat ran in a category of his own. His extraordinary performance seems to have been the result of many unlikely factors aligning. After he died, Secretariat\hich\f31506 \rquote 
\loch\f31506 s heart was reportedly measured at almost 10 kilograms \hich\f31506 \endash \loch\f31506  twi\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 ce as much as normal. This appears to have been a physiological fluke, however, as it isn\hich\f31506 \rquote 
\loch\f31506 t a trait reliably passed down to offspring. Combined with his unusual stamina, exceptionally powerful legs that allowed him to cover 25 feet per stride, favorable r\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 ac
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 e conditions, and a skilled jockey, this produced a race time that has still never been matched half a century later, despite advances in breeding, training, nutrition, and veterinary care.
\par \~
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
Secretariat offers a glimpse of the upper limits of biology. But as racehorses have pushed against that limit to become faster and more powerful, their population has become more fragile as the gene pool has narrowed. Our 4,000-year pursuit of the ultimat
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 e horse may not have much further to run.
